1
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:35,906
Oh!

2
00:02:37,074 --> 00:02:38,408
Uh!

3
00:03:19,282 --> 00:03:21,034
Ah! Uh!

4
00:04:22,345 --> 00:04:25,015
You are confused, aren't you?
Frightened.

5
00:04:25,140 --> 00:04:27,976
- That's alright, I can help you.
- Who is this?

6
00:04:28,101 --> 00:04:29,811
I am a doctor.
Now, you must listen to me.

7
00:04:29,936 --> 00:04:32,439
You have lost your memory.
There was an experiment.

8
00:04:32,564 --> 00:04:35,734
Something went wrong. Your memory
was erased. Do you understand me?

9
00:04:35,859 --> 00:04:37,694
I don't understand.
What the hell is going on here?

10
00:04:37,819 --> 00:04:40,655
Just listen. There are people
coming for you even as we speak.

11
00:04:40,781 --> 00:04:44,201
"You must not let them find you.
You must leave now.'

12
00:04:44,326 --> 00:04:46,870
Hello? Are you there?

13
00:05:45,637 --> 00:05:50,433
Hey, Mr. Murdoch, the Automat called,
said you left your wallet there.

14
00:05:51,434 --> 00:05:54,521
I suggest you retrieve it
since you only paid for three weeks,

15
00:05:54,646 --> 00:05:56,648
and they was up ten minutes ago.

16
00:05:58,275 --> 00:06:00,193
I've been here three weeks?

17
00:06:00,318 --> 00:06:03,280
It's right there in black and white,
Mr. Murdoch.

18
00:06:03,405 --> 00:06:05,156
Day and date.

19
00:06:05,824 --> 00:06:09,077
We make our books like
we make our beds, all neat and tidy.

20
00:06:09,202 --> 00:06:11,955
- I'll take care of it when I get back.
- See that you do.

21
00:06:12,080 --> 00:06:15,834
Only thing makes you a guest in this joint,
pal, is cash on the barrel head!

22
00:06:39,733 --> 00:06:42,319
House rules.
Three weeks is three weeks.

23
00:06:42,444 --> 00:06:44,863
No days off for good behavior.

24
00:07:07,010 --> 00:07:10,680
Mr. Murdoch, yes, where is he?

25
00:07:10,805 --> 00:07:14,351
He just left not five minutes ago.

26
00:07:17,562 --> 00:07:19,397
Sleep now.

27
00:07:59,813 --> 00:08:01,564
Hey, sweetie!

28
00:08:01,690 --> 00:08:05,694
He said he was your husband's doctor.
He wanted to speak with you.

29
00:08:25,714 --> 00:08:27,340
Excuse me.

30
00:08:28,675 --> 00:08:32,887
- Dr. Sh...
- Schreber. Please, won't you come in?

31
00:08:33,013 --> 00:08:35,015
You must be Emma Murdoch.

32
00:08:35,849 --> 00:08:38,893
Thank you for coming to see me
on such short notice.

33
00:08:40,937 --> 00:08:43,189
- What is this?

34
00:08:43,314 --> 00:08:47,318
A rather crude experiment,
designed to further my studies.

35
00:08:47,444 --> 00:08:51,072
I don't understand,
you say you're my husband's doctor,

36
00:08:51,197 --> 00:08:53,408
but he never mentioned you to me.

37
00:08:53,533 --> 00:08:54,951
The truth is, Mrs. Murdoch,

38
00:08:55,076 --> 00:08:57,787
John has been coming to see me
for quite some time.

39
00:08:57,912 --> 00:09:01,416
He had been grappling
with feelings of betrayal,

40
00:09:01,541 --> 00:09:04,669
stemming from your marital difficulties.

41
00:09:04,794 --> 00:09:07,005
- John told you what happened?
- Yes.

42
00:09:08,048 --> 00:09:10,258
When was the last time
that you saw him?

43
00:09:10,383 --> 00:09:11,801
Three weeks ago.

44
00:09:11,926 --> 00:09:15,555
He packed a suitcase.
He was very angry at me.

45
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:18,600
I understand how difficult
this must be for you.

46
00:09:18,725 --> 00:09:22,729
But for John's sake, I would like you
to think of me as your friend, Emma.

47
00:09:22,854 --> 00:09:26,524
It appears that John has suffered
a psychotic break.

48
00:09:26,649 --> 00:09:28,777
Complete memory loss.

49
00:09:29,736 --> 00:09:32,989
He may be delusional.
Even violent.

50
00:09:33,740 --> 00:09:39,579
Emma, if he were to contact you,
and I suspect he will,

51
00:09:39,704 --> 00:09:42,499
you must call me immediately,
do you understand?

52
00:09:42,624 --> 00:09:45,960
It is imperative that
I be the first one to reach him.

53
00:09:46,836 --> 00:09:50,840
Wherever your husband is,
he is searching...

54
00:09:50,965 --> 00:09:53,093
...for himself.

55
00:10:04,104 --> 00:10:08,942
Good evening, Mr. Murdoch.
It is Mr. Murdoch, isn't it?

56
00:10:09,067 --> 00:10:12,862
Mr...
Was it J-something-Murdoch?

57
00:10:16,032 --> 00:10:19,953
J. Murdoch.
What's your name? Justin? Jerry?

58
00:10:21,246 --> 00:10:22,664
No.

59
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:26,334
My name's Jason Murdoch.
John Murdoch.

60
00:10:26,459 --> 00:10:29,003
Jake Murdoch. How's it going?
Hi. Jack Murdoch.

61
00:10:32,423 --> 00:10:33,675
Hi.

62
00:11:13,089 --> 00:11:14,507
Bumstead.

63
00:11:16,301 --> 00:11:19,262
I told the guy,
"Cash on the barrel head."

64
00:11:19,387 --> 00:11:22,765
I said, "Listen, three weeks
is three weeks."

65
00:11:22,891 --> 00:11:27,061
- Where's our lucky winner?
- Upstairs, sir, room, uh, 614.

66
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:29,022
Another call girl.

67
00:11:30,315 --> 00:11:33,526
- "J. Murdoch.”

68
00:11:33,651 --> 00:11:36,696
- Evening, Husselbeck.
- Inspector Bumstead.

69
00:11:36,821 --> 00:11:40,200
- Your lace is untied.
- Sir.

70
00:11:51,628 --> 00:11:53,379
Am I glad you're here, sir.

71
00:11:53,504 --> 00:11:56,007
They say Detective Walenski's
got the heebie-jeebies.

72
00:11:56,132 --> 00:11:58,343
Just take what they give me,
Husselbeck.

73
00:12:05,266 --> 00:12:09,520
Wow... Looks like somebody woke up
on the wrong side of the bed.

74
00:12:52,647 --> 00:12:55,149
- Hello?
- Oh, it's you.

75
00:12:55,275 --> 00:12:57,902
- You left your wallet here, buddy.
- When did I do that?

76
00:12:58,027 --> 00:13:00,071
- When you was last here.
- When was that?

77
00:13:00,196 --> 00:13:04,033
When you left your wallet.
You expect me to remember?

78
00:13:06,911 --> 00:13:10,999
- Hello? Excuse me.

79
00:13:11,124 --> 00:13:13,793
No, no, seriously.

80
00:13:15,837 --> 00:13:17,255
Is it alleged?

81
00:13:31,352 --> 00:13:33,313
- Where you headed, chief?
- Home.

82
00:13:33,438 --> 00:13:34,689
And where's home?

83
00:13:34,814 --> 00:13:37,483
Haven't you mugs got anything
better to do, hmm?

84
00:13:37,608 --> 00:13:39,777
Well, I'm just trying to do my job, May.

85
00:13:39,902 --> 00:13:41,904
There's a killer out there,
In case you hadn't noticed.

86
00:13:42,030 --> 00:13:45,450
Maybe you should be out looking for him,
instead of cooling your heels, huh?

87
00:13:47,702 --> 00:13:49,662
Ah, go on, get out of here.

88
00:13:57,920 --> 00:13:59,005
Come on.

89
00:14:00,048 --> 00:14:02,592
Round and round she goes.

90
00:14:04,886 --> 00:14:09,015
Where she stops?
Nobody knows.

91
00:14:11,976 --> 00:14:14,937
What's that make so far, Husselbeck?
Six hookers in all?

92
00:14:15,063 --> 00:14:18,900
- I believe so, sir.
- Give the man an "A" for effort.

93
00:14:19,025 --> 00:14:21,027
You'd think these stiffs
would have the good grace

94
00:14:21,152 --> 00:14:23,780
to expire at a good time of night.

95
00:14:24,530 --> 00:14:26,783
Hey, too bad about Walenski, huh?

96
00:14:30,119 --> 00:14:31,954
I guess he'd seen enough.

97
00:14:39,087 --> 00:14:41,089
So, Husselbeck...

98
00:14:41,214 --> 00:14:47,845
what kind of killer do you think
stops to save a dying fish?

99
00:14:47,970 --> 00:14:51,057
- You got me, sir.
- Bumstead, what took you?

100
00:14:51,182 --> 00:14:53,935
This killer's been running circles
around us, thanks to Walenski.

101
00:14:54,060 --> 00:14:55,770
With all due respect,
Chief Inspector,

102
00:14:55,895 --> 00:14:59,190
I've known Eddie a long time,
he's a good cop.

103
00:14:59,315 --> 00:15:03,444
Whatever kind of cop Walenski once was,
he let drift a long time ago.

104
00:15:03,569 --> 00:15:06,406
- Let me talk to Bumstead!
- Come on, let's go!

105
00:15:06,531 --> 00:15:09,200
- Hold on!
- Frank! Frank!

106
00:15:10,535 --> 00:15:12,703
- Whoal
- Get his arm!

107
00:15:12,829 --> 00:15:14,831
- They're watching us!
- On your feet.

108
00:15:14,956 --> 00:15:18,000
- There's no way out!
- Don't make us hurt you.

109
00:15:18,126 --> 00:15:19,752
God, can't you see?

110
00:15:23,172 --> 00:15:25,967
Let me go, let me go!
I'm not gonna hurt you. I'm not...

111
00:15:26,926 --> 00:15:29,262
- What was that?
- Walenski.

112
00:15:37,687 --> 00:15:40,773
I'm being punished for my sins, right?

113
00:15:40,898 --> 00:15:44,318
What did I ever do to inherit this?
Where do you begin?

114
00:15:45,069 --> 00:15:49,282
Well, everything Detective Walenski
committed to paper should be here, so...

115
00:15:49,407 --> 00:15:52,910
The only thing that should be committed
is Walenski.

116
00:15:54,579 --> 00:15:57,623
Nothing like a little healthy paranoia.

117
00:16:00,251 --> 00:16:02,712
Sir, we just got
the fingerprint results in.

118
00:16:06,674 --> 00:16:08,676
What is this, some kind of joke?

119
00:16:12,805 --> 00:16:14,807
Don't throw anything away.

120
00:16:20,146 --> 00:16:21,522
Inspector Bumstead.

121
00:16:23,649 --> 00:16:26,527
I came here to file a missing persons
report for my husband.

122
00:16:26,652 --> 00:16:31,073
- Take it to the front desk.
- They told me to come see you.

123
00:16:32,867 --> 00:16:34,869
His name's John Murdoch.

124
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:39,415
Mrs. Murdoch,
why didn't you report this before?

125
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:44,295
If your husband has been missing
as long as you say...

126
00:16:44,420 --> 00:16:46,672
I thought he'd simply walked out on me.

127
00:16:48,007 --> 00:16:51,344
Then a doctor contacted me
this evening.

128
00:16:52,303 --> 00:16:55,223
- How long have you been married?
- Nearly four years.

129
00:16:55,348 --> 00:16:57,892
- Hmm.
- Why do you ask?

130
00:16:58,017 --> 00:17:00,853
Because you seem uncomfortable
with your ring.

131
00:17:00,978 --> 00:17:04,315
As if you were...
unaccustomed to wearing it.

132
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:06,484
I never take it off.

133
00:17:12,782 --> 00:17:16,118
Do these names mean
anything to you?

134
00:17:22,291 --> 00:17:24,335
No.
Who are these women?

135
00:17:25,169 --> 00:17:29,257
Why are you looking for my husband?
Are you going to accuse him of something?

136
00:17:29,382 --> 00:17:31,842
Maybe. Maybe murder.

137
00:17:31,968 --> 00:17:34,262
Whose murder?

138
00:17:34,387 --> 00:17:38,015
- Which one?
- Ha-ha! All of them.

139
00:17:38,975 --> 00:17:42,144
Mrs. Murdoch, I am sorry.
I'm sorry, I didn't mean to alarm you.

140
00:17:42,270 --> 00:17:44,647
I'm sorry that I came.
Just both made a mistake.

141
00:17:44,772 --> 00:17:46,399
Mrs. Murdoch, if you please.

142
00:17:46,524 --> 00:17:48,859
If you'll just give me
half a second to explain...

143
00:17:50,945 --> 00:17:53,197
- I'll be right out.

144
00:18:17,430 --> 00:18:19,682
Have we met before?

145
00:18:19,807 --> 00:18:23,352
If we did,
I hope you're still breathing.

146
00:18:23,477 --> 00:18:26,647
What's your name, honey?

147
00:18:26,772 --> 00:18:30,568
- John. Ha-ha!
- Ha-ha! Well, that's an appropriate name.

148
00:18:31,944 --> 00:18:33,571
Yeah.

149
00:18:38,451 --> 00:18:40,328
I was just thinking...

150
00:18:43,831 --> 00:18:45,583
What you do...

151
00:18:46,667 --> 00:18:51,547
...seems kind of dangerous,
right now.

152
00:18:51,672 --> 00:18:53,924
I mean, how do you know
I'm not the killer?

153
00:18:54,050 --> 00:18:57,136
You don't seem like the killer type.
Why?

154
00:18:58,387 --> 00:19:00,890
You feeling any urges
I should know about?

155
00:19:22,161 --> 00:19:23,996
Ah, shoot.

156
00:19:51,565 --> 00:19:53,317
Oh!

157
00:20:22,388 --> 00:20:26,392
So, it seems you've discovered
your unpleasant nature.

158
00:20:29,603 --> 00:20:34,316
- Who are you?
- We might ask the same question, yes?

159
00:20:34,442 --> 00:20:36,944
Sleep now.

160
00:20:59,467 --> 00:21:01,010
Aargh!

161
00:21:03,345 --> 00:21:05,973
- He can tune!

162
00:21:57,650 --> 00:22:00,861
What is to be done?
This man is dangerous.

163
00:22:00,986 --> 00:22:04,406
- It is said he is able to tune.
- Impossible.

164
00:22:04,532 --> 00:22:07,243
We have seen it
with our own eyes.

165
00:22:07,368 --> 00:22:09,787
On occasion,
the imprinting does not take.

166
00:22:09,912 --> 00:22:12,456
They behave erratically
when they awaken.

167
00:22:12,581 --> 00:22:16,126
We find them wandering
like lost children.

168
00:22:16,252 --> 00:22:18,587
But this one was different, yes?

169
00:22:18,712 --> 00:22:22,341
- What has the Doctor to say about this?
- He has failed to report in.

170
00:22:22,466 --> 00:22:25,511
- And Mr. Quick?
- No more Mr. Quick.

171
00:22:25,636 --> 00:22:28,305
Mr. Quick, dead, yes.

172
00:22:31,350 --> 00:22:34,186
Poor, poor Mr. Quick.

173
00:22:34,311 --> 00:22:37,106
Mr. Book, does he know?

174
00:22:38,482 --> 00:22:41,026
Should we not know, Mr. Hand?

175
00:22:41,151 --> 00:22:44,113
We had hoped to learn more
before sharing with you.

176
00:22:44,238 --> 00:22:47,074
We can know nothing
until we possess him.

177
00:22:47,992 --> 00:22:50,703
Mr. Night, you will take the East.

178
00:22:50,828 --> 00:22:53,247
Mr. Face, you the West.

179
00:22:53,372 --> 00:22:55,374
Mr. Glove, the South.

180
00:22:55,499 --> 00:22:57,960
Mr. Shade, the North.

181
00:22:59,545 --> 00:23:02,464
We must have this man.

182
00:23:15,978 --> 00:23:21,108
Do you really think my husband could be
capable of committing those murders?

183
00:23:21,233 --> 00:23:22,735
Do you?

184
00:23:24,445 --> 00:23:27,031
Let's hope, for both our sakes,
that I'm wrong.

185
00:23:56,060 --> 00:23:57,603
John?

186
00:24:11,909 --> 00:24:14,078
I've been so worried about you.

187
00:24:17,539 --> 00:24:19,583
Are you punishing me?

188
00:24:19,708 --> 00:24:23,253
You know, if disappearing is your way
of punishing me, I don't appreciate it!

189
00:24:23,379 --> 00:24:25,422
I don't know what you're talking about.

190
00:24:28,133 --> 00:24:32,137
I found these keys in my pocket,
so I assume I live here.

191
00:24:33,973 --> 00:24:36,475
- You supposed to be my wife?
- Supposed to be?

192
00:24:37,559 --> 00:24:38,852
John!

193
00:24:39,937 --> 00:24:44,108
You really... don't know
who I am, do you?

194
00:24:45,067 --> 00:24:47,486
Your doctor called me.
He was worried that this might happen.

195
00:24:47,611 --> 00:24:49,488
My doctor?

196
00:24:49,613 --> 00:24:53,951
Yeah.
He, uh... he gave me his card.

197
00:24:54,076 --> 00:24:56,537
He's desperate to find you.

198
00:24:57,538 --> 00:25:00,332
Schreber...

199
00:25:03,460 --> 00:25:05,421
I feel like I'm living out
someone else's nightmare.

200
00:25:05,546 --> 00:25:08,298
What happened to me?
Why was I seeing a doctor?

201
00:25:09,049 --> 00:25:11,218
I had an affair.

202
00:25:12,302 --> 00:25:14,680
You were angry at me.

203
00:25:16,974 --> 00:25:18,976
The police are looking for you.

204
00:25:19,101 --> 00:25:21,020
I know, I saw the papers.

205
00:25:21,145 --> 00:25:24,023
"Killer Stalks City Streetwalkers."

206
00:25:27,484 --> 00:25:33,240
I was with one of those women,
before I came here tonight.

207
00:25:33,365 --> 00:25:36,702
- I don't understand.
- I met her outside an Automat.

208
00:25:37,703 --> 00:25:39,788
I guess I wanted to test myself.

209
00:25:41,415 --> 00:25:44,710
I wanted to know if I had it in me
to do those things.

210
00:25:44,835 --> 00:25:49,381
Maybe I have lost my mind, but whoever I am,
I'm still me and I'm not a killer.

211
00:25:55,387 --> 00:25:57,139
I believe you.

212
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:00,059
You do?

213
00:26:03,937 --> 00:26:04,980
What?

214
00:26:05,105 --> 00:26:07,816
The car outside,
the one you arrived in.

215
00:26:07,941 --> 00:26:10,360
It's a cop.
He dropped me off here.

216
00:26:12,362 --> 00:26:14,948
I didn't know he was still here.

217
00:26:15,074 --> 00:26:16,492
John!

218
00:26:17,409 --> 00:26:20,037
Hold it right there, Mister!

219
00:26:21,205 --> 00:26:23,415
Wait.
You have the wrong man.

220
00:26:23,540 --> 00:26:25,876
- Stand aside, will you, please?
- I didn't kill anyone.

221
00:26:26,001 --> 00:26:29,588
Right now, what you are is a suspect.
Turn yourself in.

222
00:26:29,713 --> 00:26:31,548
I'll listen to whatever
you have to say.

223
00:26:31,673 --> 00:26:34,551
You're not gonna believe
what I have to say.

224
00:26:34,676 --> 00:26:38,180
- Try me.
- There's someone after me.

225
00:26:38,305 --> 00:26:41,558
There's this group of men,
they want me dead.

226
00:26:41,683 --> 00:26:44,728
And I don't even know if...
they're not even...

227
00:26:44,853 --> 00:26:48,357
- Yeah, who's gonna listen to a madman?
- Stand aside, Mrs. Murdoch.

228
00:26:48,482 --> 00:26:49,983
Run!

229
00:26:53,904 --> 00:26:56,532
No one ever listens to me.

230
00:27:01,787 --> 00:27:03,205
Murdoch, stop!

231
00:27:08,127 --> 00:27:09,628
Murdoch!

232
00:27:45,956 --> 00:27:48,417
- How much further?
- We're almost there, buddy.

233
00:27:51,003 --> 00:27:54,089
Hey, you happen to know the way
to Shell Beach?

234
00:27:54,214 --> 00:27:57,426
You're kidding. Me and the missus
spent our honeymoon there.

235
00:27:57,551 --> 00:28:00,179
All you gotta do
is take Main Street West to...

236
00:28:02,723 --> 00:28:04,725
Or is it the cross...?

237
00:28:04,850 --> 00:28:06,852
You know, that's funny,

238
00:28:06,977 --> 00:28:10,731
I can't seem to remember
if it's Main Street West or the crosstown.

239
00:28:15,319 --> 00:28:18,572
I'm sorry, Inspector,
I cannot be of more assistance,

240
00:28:18,697 --> 00:28:20,532
but I am running late
for an appointment.

241
00:28:20,657 --> 00:28:23,076
Well, I appreciate
your taking the time.

242
00:28:31,418 --> 00:28:33,629
There's just one thing
that puzzles me.

243
00:28:33,754 --> 00:28:38,383
I've met quite a few murderers
in the course of my work.

244
00:28:38,508 --> 00:28:41,094
Murdoch doesn't strike me as one.

245
00:28:41,220 --> 00:28:44,181
Perhaps you are not accustomed
to digging deep enough.

246
00:28:44,306 --> 00:28:47,351
Well, I do know when someone
is lying to me, Doctor.

247
00:28:47,476 --> 00:28:50,020
Forgive me, Inspector,
but you are not a clinician.

248
00:28:50,145 --> 00:28:53,815
Judging personalities
happens to be my business.

249
00:28:53,941 --> 00:28:55,859
Well, maybe you could give me
a few pointers.

250
00:28:55,984 --> 00:28:57,611
Certainly.

251
00:28:57,736 --> 00:28:59,821
Let's take you, for instance.

252
00:28:59,947 --> 00:29:05,160
You are a fastidious man,
driven, consumed by details.

253
00:29:06,119 --> 00:29:08,080
I would say your life is...

254
00:29:09,373 --> 00:29:10,958
...rather lonely.

255
00:29:11,083 --> 00:29:13,627
Good evening, Inspector.
I really must be going.

256
00:29:55,085 --> 00:29:59,006
'Closing time.
The pool is now closed.'

257
00:30:05,971 --> 00:30:07,139
Dr. Schreber.

258
00:30:08,682 --> 00:30:13,478
Most unfortunate it is that
we were forced to seek you out here.

259
00:30:14,396 --> 00:30:18,692
You know how uncomfortable
we find all this... moisture.

260
00:30:19,484 --> 00:30:22,738
- I'm sorry, ...
- Failed to report in, yes.

261
00:30:22,863 --> 00:30:25,407
I was frightened.
I have a weak heart, you know.

262
00:30:25,532 --> 00:30:29,077
Your weakness is not, we think,
an affair of the heart.

263
00:30:29,202 --> 00:30:31,997
We found this in his hotel room.

264
00:30:32,122 --> 00:30:35,542
Must we reproduce
Mr. Murdoch's memories again?

265
00:30:35,667 --> 00:30:37,085
I tried to imprint him,

266
00:30:37,210 --> 00:30:39,212
but he woke up, he knocked
the syringe right out of my hand.

267
00:30:39,338 --> 00:30:41,381
I tried to stop him,
but he was too fast.

268
00:30:41,506 --> 00:30:44,134
- He has no memories, then?
- Only fragments.

269
00:30:44,259 --> 00:30:45,969
The procedure, it was interrupted.

270
00:30:46,094 --> 00:30:49,264
I guess it's only a matter
of rounding him up.

271
00:30:49,389 --> 00:30:53,477
- You have had strays before, right?
- This is no stray, Doctor.

272
00:30:53,602 --> 00:30:55,103
This one can tune.

273
00:30:55,228 --> 00:30:58,065
But that's impossible.
I thought only you had that ability.

274
00:30:58,190 --> 00:31:02,652
You will process another template
of the subject's memories, yes?

275
00:31:02,778 --> 00:31:06,031
- Of course. I'll imprint him again.
- No.

276
00:31:06,156 --> 00:31:09,534
We require them
for a quite different purpose.

277
00:31:09,659 --> 00:31:12,287
It's almost midnight, Dr. Schreber.

278
00:31:12,412 --> 00:31:17,125
We'll talk again after tonight's tuning,
but no more delays, yes?

279
00:31:17,250 --> 00:31:21,963
No more... inconsistencies
in your behavior.

280
00:31:29,513 --> 00:31:31,515
You appear quite frail, Doctor.

281
00:31:32,933 --> 00:31:35,811
Perhaps some exercise
would do you good.

282
00:31:50,283 --> 00:31:52,285
- Come In.
- Kate.

283
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:58,625
- How is he?
- The same.

284
00:31:59,418 --> 00:32:02,129
Walenski? It's me, Frank.

285
00:32:08,510 --> 00:32:10,178
Come in, Frank.

286
00:32:12,806 --> 00:32:14,474
Close the door.

287
00:32:22,232 --> 00:32:25,360
I've been looking through
some of your old reports.

288
00:32:25,485 --> 00:32:27,571
It's an interesting case.

289
00:32:28,697 --> 00:32:31,950
The kind that can make
a man's career.

290
00:32:33,702 --> 00:32:37,247
- Or break it.
- Yeah, I was on that case.

291
00:32:37,372 --> 00:32:41,126
And then, what?
What happened then, Eddie?

292
00:32:41,251 --> 00:32:43,003
Nothing happened, Frank.

293
00:32:43,128 --> 00:32:47,591
I've just been spending time
on the subway, riding in circles.

294
00:32:47,716 --> 00:32:51,470
Thinking in circles.
There's no way out.

295
00:32:53,346 --> 00:32:55,182
I've been over every inch of this city.

296
00:32:59,978 --> 00:33:01,980
You're scaring your wife to death,
Eddie.

297
00:33:02,105 --> 00:33:03,982
She's not my wife.

298
00:33:05,317 --> 00:33:08,236
I don't know who she is.
I don't know who any of us are.

299
00:33:08,361 --> 00:33:10,322
What makes you say that?

300
00:33:10,447 --> 00:33:12,782
Do you think about
the past much, Frank?

301
00:33:13,867 --> 00:33:16,244
As much as the next guy.

302
00:33:16,369 --> 00:33:18,955
See, I've been trying
to remember things,

303
00:33:19,080 --> 00:33:22,000
clearly remember things
from my past.

304
00:33:22,125 --> 00:33:27,380
But the more I try to think back,
the more it all starts to unravel.

305
00:33:27,506 --> 00:33:32,802
None of it seems real,
it's like I've just been dreaming this life,

306
00:33:32,928 --> 00:33:35,847
and when I finally wake up,
I'll be somebody else.

307
00:33:35,972 --> 00:33:37,682
Somebody totally different.

308
00:33:42,479 --> 00:33:45,565
You saw something, didn't you, Eddie?

309
00:33:45,690 --> 00:33:48,318
Something to do with the case.

310
00:33:48,443 --> 00:33:52,239
There is no case!
There never was!

311
00:33:52,364 --> 00:33:56,701
It's all just a big joke! It's a joke!

312
00:34:37,659 --> 00:34:39,661
<i>"Tonight's requirements are:</i>

313
00:34:39,786 --> 00:34:44,791
'twelve family photo albums,
nine personal diaries,

314
00:34:44,916 --> 00:34:49,671
'seventeen love letters,
assorted childhood photographs,

315
00:34:49,796 --> 00:34:53,800
'twenty-six wallets,
ID's and social security cards...'

316
00:34:53,925 --> 00:34:56,011
These do bring back memories.

317
00:34:58,305 --> 00:35:00,599
This one is still warm.

318
00:35:01,308 --> 00:35:05,687
What is it?
The recollections of a great lover?

319
00:35:05,812 --> 00:35:10,275
A catalog of conquests?
We will soon find out.

320
00:35:11,526 --> 00:35:16,281
You wouldn't appreciate that, would you,
Mr. whatever-your-name-is?

321
00:35:16,406 --> 00:35:20,702
Not the sort of conquests
you would ever understand.

322
00:35:21,536 --> 00:35:22,704
Let's see.

323
00:35:24,080 --> 00:35:26,708
A touch of unhappy childhood.

324
00:35:28,960 --> 00:35:30,211
Ah...

325
00:35:32,422 --> 00:35:35,258
A dash of teenage rebellion.

326
00:35:36,885 --> 00:35:39,095
And last but not least...

327
00:35:39,846 --> 00:35:41,765
...a tragic death in the family.

328
00:35:41,890 --> 00:35:43,141
Doctor.

329
00:35:45,143 --> 00:35:46,353
Mr. Book.

330
00:35:48,063 --> 00:35:49,731
Whoa!

331
00:35:49,856 --> 00:35:51,733
Ah! Ah!

332
00:35:53,777 --> 00:35:57,489
Why does Murdoch not sleep
during the tuning as the others do?

333
00:35:57,614 --> 00:36:01,618
I don't know. Maybe he's a step
up the evolutionary ladder.

334
00:36:01,743 --> 00:36:04,454
A freak of nature.
He's adapting to survive.

335
00:36:04,579 --> 00:36:07,916
What do you expect?
Weren't you looking for the human soul?

336
00:36:08,041 --> 00:36:10,543
That's the purpose
of your little zoo, isn't it?

337
00:36:10,669 --> 00:36:13,922
That's why you keep changing people
and things around every night.

338
00:36:14,047 --> 00:36:16,675
Maybe you have finally found
what you are looking for,

339
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:19,260
and it's going to bite you
on your... Aargh!

340
00:36:22,389 --> 00:36:25,975
It requires several of your lifetimes
to master our gifts.

341
00:36:26,101 --> 00:36:29,854
The idea that a simple man
could develop the ability to tune is...

342
00:36:29,979 --> 00:36:33,566
Absurd, I know.
But what other explanation is there?

343
00:36:38,405 --> 00:36:40,073
It is time.

344
00:37:25,368 --> 00:37:26,870
Shut it down.

345
00:37:38,798 --> 00:37:40,300
Hey, wake up!

346
00:37:40,425 --> 00:37:43,887
- Get your paper right here!

347
00:37:55,315 --> 00:38:00,069
It's driving me crazy. I can't sleep.
And they call my job "unskilled".

348
00:38:00,195 --> 00:38:04,365
You think you've got it tough? You try
looking after these kids for a change!

349
00:38:04,491 --> 00:38:09,078
Anyways, Frederickson says he'll take me
off the damn night shift soon.

350
00:38:09,204 --> 00:38:11,164
Well, it's about time, dear.

351
00:39:26,781 --> 00:39:27,949
Hello?

352
00:39:30,660 --> 00:39:31,911
Hello?

353
00:39:36,207 --> 00:39:38,585
Wake up! Hey!

354
00:39:41,045 --> 00:39:43,673
Wake up! Wake up!

355
00:39:46,009 --> 00:39:47,844
Wake up!

356
00:39:49,178 --> 00:39:50,597
Hello?

357
00:39:52,265 --> 00:39:55,435
- Can anyone hear me?

358
00:39:57,562 --> 00:40:00,565
Let the tuning commence.

359
00:42:40,224 --> 00:42:43,603
The Goodwins. Jeremy, Sylvia.

360
00:42:43,728 --> 00:42:45,897
Jane and little Matthew.

361
00:42:49,734 --> 00:42:51,819
Jane and little Matthew.

362
00:43:21,974 --> 00:43:24,227
The rich get richer.

363
00:43:24,352 --> 00:43:27,563
Probably have maid's quarters
before the night is through.

364
00:44:21,367 --> 00:44:23,286
Dr. Schreber, I presume.

365
00:44:23,953 --> 00:44:26,164
- It's you!!
- Hey, come on.

366
00:44:26,289 --> 00:44:30,293
That's no way to greet a patient, Doctor.
If that's what you are, huh, Doctor?

367
00:44:30,418 --> 00:44:31,794
Mr. Murdoch, please, I...

368
00:44:31,919 --> 00:44:33,629
What is happening here?
Why is everyone asleep?

369
00:44:33,754 --> 00:44:36,048
Shh!
Please keep your voice down.

370
00:44:36,174 --> 00:44:38,009
Why can't I remember anything?
What have you done to me?

371
00:44:38,134 --> 00:44:40,803
Nothing, nothing.
Please, I want to help you.

372
00:44:40,928 --> 00:44:44,390
We cannot talk here. It is not safe.
If they see us together, they'll...

373
00:44:44,515 --> 00:44:47,226
I don't care. I want some answers.
I want some answers now!

374
00:44:47,351 --> 00:44:50,396
Who are they?
Why are they trying to kill me?

375
00:44:50,521 --> 00:44:51,606
<i>Answer me!</i>

376
00:44:56,777 --> 00:45:01,324
- My god, you really can do it.
- I did that?

377
00:45:01,449 --> 00:45:05,453
Listen to me, John. You have their power.
You can make things happen by will alone.

378
00:45:05,578 --> 00:45:09,081
They call it "tuning". That is how
they make the buildings change.

379
00:45:09,207 --> 00:45:11,667
Just now, you acted
out of self-defense, a reflex.

380
00:45:11,792 --> 00:45:14,462
But I can teach you to control
your power consciously.

381
00:45:14,587 --> 00:45:16,797
Let me help you, John.
Together we can stop them.

382
00:45:16,923 --> 00:45:19,926
- We can take the city back.

383
00:45:21,844 --> 00:45:23,846
- My glasses.

384
00:45:24,972 --> 00:45:27,016
My glasses, please!

385
00:46:14,021 --> 00:46:16,440
He made a terrible impression
for the firm,

386
00:46:16,565 --> 00:46:19,151
lollygagging around as he did.

387
00:46:19,277 --> 00:46:22,071
So, I simply told Frederickson
I was going to have to let him go.

388
00:46:22,196 --> 00:46:24,824
And well you should have, darling.

389
00:46:24,949 --> 00:46:26,534
He attacked me.

390
00:46:27,368 --> 00:46:30,997
- I don't know where he has gone.
- Come, Dr. Schreber.

391
00:46:32,373 --> 00:46:35,251
We have much to do, yes?

392
00:46:36,585 --> 00:46:37,878
Yes.

393
00:48:28,864 --> 00:48:32,535
Tonight's experiment shall be conducted
in the following locations.

394
00:48:32,660 --> 00:48:36,497
The first subject shall be imprinted
here on Avenue M.

395
00:48:36,622 --> 00:48:39,583
Mr. Book, there is a-a-a problem.

396
00:48:39,708 --> 00:48:42,962
There is no Avenue M.
We were unable to complete it.

397
00:48:43,087 --> 00:48:46,507
During the last tuning,
we detected a lack of control.

398
00:48:46,632 --> 00:48:49,427
An opposing influence
on the machines?

399
00:48:49,552 --> 00:48:53,848
Then, this man Murdoch is
more powerful than we thought.

400
00:48:53,973 --> 00:48:56,016
He's becoming like us.

401
00:48:56,142 --> 00:48:59,603
So we must become like him.

402
00:49:00,438 --> 00:49:02,690
The good doctor has done
as we asked?

403
00:49:02,815 --> 00:49:06,444
The life and times
of John Murdoch, Volume 2.

404
00:49:08,612 --> 00:49:11,073
We must not do this.

405
00:49:11,198 --> 00:49:14,660
Murdoch does not possess
these memories, yes?

406
00:49:14,785 --> 00:49:17,371
How will his imprint allow us
to find him?

407
00:49:17,496 --> 00:49:21,917
Everywhere he goes, everyone
he seeks out, will be known to us.

408
00:49:22,042 --> 00:49:27,465
And as he follows the clues,
so shall we follow the memories.

409
00:49:27,590 --> 00:49:30,134
Perhaps we have forgotten
what happened last time, when we...

410
00:49:30,259 --> 00:49:33,304
Yes. Poor, poor Mr...

411
00:49:33,429 --> 00:49:35,222
We have not forgotten.

412
00:49:35,347 --> 00:49:42,521
If Mr. Hand wishes to make this sacrifice
for the greater good,

413
00:49:42,646 --> 00:49:44,398
so be it.

414
00:49:44,523 --> 00:49:49,820
But to imprint one of us...
Failure has always resulted.

415
00:49:49,945 --> 00:49:53,407
What Mr. Hand proposes
Is our only option.

416
00:49:53,532 --> 00:49:55,034
Imprint.

417
00:50:00,289 --> 00:50:02,291
This may sting a bit.

418
00:50:38,994 --> 00:50:40,996
Is it done?

419
00:50:41,622 --> 00:50:43,541
Oh, yes, Mr. Book.

420
00:50:45,042 --> 00:50:48,754
I have John Murdoch in mind.

421
00:50:53,425 --> 00:50:58,639
"Johnny, I found this postcard
among your mother's things.

422
00:50:58,764 --> 00:51:02,768
"Brings back memories, doesn't it?
Stop by sometime.

423
00:51:02,893 --> 00:51:06,146
"We'll see if we can't hook ourselves
another mermaid.

424
00:51:06,272 --> 00:51:08,274
"Love to Emma. Karl."

425
00:51:13,654 --> 00:51:14,655
Karl.

426
00:51:16,949 --> 00:51:18,659
Karl Harris.

427
00:51:26,834 --> 00:51:29,461
Harris... Harris...

428
00:51:32,339 --> 00:51:34,133
Harris, Karl.

429
00:51:36,135 --> 00:51:38,470
Excuse me, can you tell me
the way to Avenue C?

430
00:51:39,138 --> 00:51:40,472
Try the subway.

431
00:51:44,768 --> 00:51:49,023
- You got a problem, pal?
- You, uh... been working here long?

432
00:51:49,982 --> 00:51:53,319
Twenty-five years.
No days off for good behavior.

433
00:52:18,636 --> 00:52:22,890
- We're looking for John Murdoch.

434
00:52:53,253 --> 00:52:55,631
- Shh!

435
00:52:55,756 --> 00:52:57,758
She knows nothing,
Mr. Hand.

436
00:52:57,883 --> 00:52:59,551
A dead end, yes, Mr. Wall?

437
00:52:59,677 --> 00:53:02,388
We thought his imprint would allow us
to track him, yes?

438
00:53:02,513 --> 00:53:05,015
But instead,
we have been brought here.

439
00:53:05,140 --> 00:53:09,061
- This is irrational.
- Instincts are irrational, Mr. Wall.

440
00:53:09,186 --> 00:53:12,398
And we must follow
where they lead, yes?

441
00:53:12,523 --> 00:53:17,069
Mr. Sleep suggests he may go
to places familiar... his job.

442
00:53:17,194 --> 00:53:19,154
He does not care about our job.

443
00:53:19,279 --> 00:53:21,907
Indulge us, Mr. Hand.

444
00:53:22,032 --> 00:53:24,702
If you were Murdoch, yes?

445
00:53:24,827 --> 00:53:28,288
If... I were Murdoch...

446
00:53:29,957 --> 00:53:36,463
...I would remember how my wife had
hurt me by sleeping with another man.

447
00:53:37,464 --> 00:53:43,595
And then... I would look for a way
to hurt her in return.

448
00:53:46,932 --> 00:53:49,768
- Leave me alone with her.

449
00:53:49,893 --> 00:53:51,603
There's work to be done.

450
00:53:55,065 --> 00:53:57,401
'All change, platform three.

451
00:53:57,526 --> 00:54:01,363
- 'Platform three, all change.'
- All change?

452
00:54:01,488 --> 00:54:03,365
Excuse me. How do I get
to the end of the line?

453
00:54:03,490 --> 00:54:04,950
You want the Express.

454
00:54:12,624 --> 00:54:15,961
'...train leaving on platform seven.'

455
00:54:16,086 --> 00:54:17,421
- Platform two.
- Thanks a lot.

456
00:54:17,546 --> 00:54:21,467
- Hey! How come that train didn't stop?
- That's the Express.

457
00:54:22,843 --> 00:54:24,720
There's no way out, you know?

458
00:54:24,845 --> 00:54:28,515
You can't get out of the city.
Believe me, I've tried.

459
00:54:28,640 --> 00:54:32,478
You're Murdoch, aren't you?
The one they've been looking for.

460
00:54:32,603 --> 00:54:35,063
- Who are you?
- Used to be a cop.

461
00:54:35,189 --> 00:54:37,691
At least, in this life, I was.

462
00:54:37,816 --> 00:54:40,194
They steal people's memories,
you know?

463
00:54:40,319 --> 00:54:42,321
Then, they swap them around
between us.

464
00:54:42,446 --> 00:54:44,948
I've seen them do it,
back and forth, back and forth,

465
00:54:45,073 --> 00:54:47,159
till no one knows
who they are anymore.

466
00:54:47,284 --> 00:54:48,660
How do you know all this?

467
00:54:48,786 --> 00:54:52,831
Once in a while, one of us wakes up
while they're changing things.

468
00:54:52,956 --> 00:54:55,375
It's not supposed to happen,
but it does.

469
00:54:55,501 --> 00:54:57,419
It happened to me.

470
00:54:57,544 --> 00:55:00,464
They'll come looking for you,
Murdoch.

471
00:55:00,589 --> 00:55:02,841
Just like they'll come looking for me!

472
00:55:02,966 --> 00:55:05,761
But that's okay,
I figured a way out!

473
00:55:17,231 --> 00:55:19,399
They were here.

474
00:55:20,484 --> 00:55:23,987
- Last night.
- This was to be his home.

475
00:55:25,447 --> 00:55:28,617
Ah...
That was when we loved her.

476
00:55:28,742 --> 00:55:30,994
This is all well and good,
Mr. Hand,

477
00:55:31,119 --> 00:55:35,082
but we require a more practical link
to his present whereabouts.

478
00:55:37,793 --> 00:55:38,961
Yes.

479
00:55:39,837 --> 00:55:42,506
And I know where to find her.

480
00:55:50,430 --> 00:55:54,393
- We're very lucky, when you think about it.
- I'm sorry?

481
00:55:54,518 --> 00:55:59,356
To be able to revisit those places
which have meant so very much to us.

482
00:55:59,481 --> 00:56:01,608
I thought it was more that
we were haunted by them.

483
00:56:01,733 --> 00:56:03,151
Perhaps.

484
00:56:05,195 --> 00:56:08,949
But imagine a life alien to yours,

485
00:56:09,783 --> 00:56:11,660
in which your memories
were not your own

486
00:56:11,785 --> 00:56:14,621
but those shared
by every other of your kind.

487
00:56:15,998 --> 00:56:19,001
Imagine the torment
of such an existence.

488
00:56:20,377 --> 00:56:23,297
No experiences to call your own.

489
00:56:25,299 --> 00:56:29,553
If it was all you knew,
maybe it would be a comfort.

490
00:56:30,262 --> 00:56:32,890
But if you were to discover
something different...

491
00:56:34,141 --> 00:56:36,518
...something better.

492
00:56:40,689 --> 00:56:43,108
There used to be a ferry
when I was a boy.

493
00:56:43,233 --> 00:56:48,322
Biggest thing you ever saw,
lit up like a floating birthday cake.

494
00:56:49,448 --> 00:56:53,619
That's just what my husband
once said to me, on this very spot.

495
00:56:54,995 --> 00:56:56,830
Where is your husband now?

496
00:56:56,955 --> 00:57:00,334
I wish I knew.
What brings you here?

497
00:57:01,168 --> 00:57:04,087
I met my wife at this place.

498
00:57:04,212 --> 00:57:08,133
- It's where I first met my husband.
- Small world.

499
00:57:28,904 --> 00:57:32,324
Ha-ha! No, no, no!

500
00:58:01,186 --> 00:58:02,938
Hello?

501
00:58:04,147 --> 00:58:05,565
Hello?

502
00:58:40,976 --> 00:58:42,811
Oh! Ah!

503
00:58:50,652 --> 00:58:54,031
- What are you doing here?
- I've been following you.

504
00:58:57,868 --> 00:58:59,578
Hold it right there!

505
00:59:01,621 --> 00:59:04,750
Johnny? Johnny!

506
00:59:05,792 --> 00:59:09,504
It's been so damn long,
I thought you'd forgotten your Uncle Karl.

507
00:59:10,338 --> 00:59:12,758
You son of a gun!

508
00:59:15,177 --> 00:59:16,595
Uncle Karl...

509
00:59:20,140 --> 00:59:21,475
Wait.

510
00:59:23,852 --> 00:59:26,104
- Is that me?
- Yeah.

511
00:59:35,155 --> 00:59:37,199
Shell Beach is where I grew up?

512
00:59:37,324 --> 00:59:40,786
That's what I said.
Shell Beach, your hometown.

513
00:59:40,911 --> 00:59:43,371
I need to get there, Karl.
How do I get there?

514
00:59:44,539 --> 00:59:47,250
I don't know.
I haven't been there in years.

515
00:59:47,375 --> 00:59:50,837
Come on, you must remember.
You gotta have some idea.

516
00:59:50,962 --> 00:59:53,590
- Come on!
- Uh, sorry, Johnny.

517
00:59:55,217 --> 00:59:58,095
The old cracker barrel ain't
what it used to be.

518
01:00:01,515 --> 01:00:05,560
- So bright there.
- Brighter times, I guess.

519
01:00:07,312 --> 01:00:09,648
You were always scribbling
in that damn book.

520
01:00:10,398 --> 01:00:14,861
That's me and your pa.
Ha! What a couple of handsome fools!

521
01:00:16,196 --> 01:00:18,782
What happened to my parents?

522
01:00:22,369 --> 01:00:24,412
Where are they now?

523
01:00:24,538 --> 01:00:28,959
They're dead, Johnny.
They died when their house burnt down.

524
01:00:29,084 --> 01:00:33,588
I looked after you.
You don't remember that, really?

525
01:00:36,758 --> 01:00:38,176
What's that?

526
01:00:38,301 --> 01:00:40,554
- Huh?
- What is that?

527
01:00:45,433 --> 01:00:47,102
It's a scar.

528
01:00:47,227 --> 01:00:49,688
You burnt your arm pretty bad
in the fire.

529
01:00:55,318 --> 01:00:58,530
- What does it mean, Johnny?
- It means these are all lies!

530
01:01:02,200 --> 01:01:04,411
Why are you here,
Mrs. Murdoch?'

531
01:01:13,461 --> 01:01:16,047
My husband told me
he'd been here.

532
01:01:19,718 --> 01:01:23,305
He told me he wanted
to test himself,

533
01:01:23,430 --> 01:01:27,767
see If he was capable of...

534
01:01:36,902 --> 01:01:40,739
I wanted to talk to her. I thought
maybe she could help me find him.

535
01:01:42,490 --> 01:01:44,910
Stay here.
I'm gonna call the station.

536
01:01:55,587 --> 01:02:00,884
Yeah, this is Bumstead. Send the homicide
coroner to 1440 East, yeah.

537
01:02:01,009 --> 01:02:02,761
There's been another one.

538
01:02:03,803 --> 01:02:06,640
It's the same pattern.
I'll explain when you get here.

539
01:02:17,317 --> 01:02:19,194
Oh, god.

540
01:02:19,319 --> 01:02:22,364
It's okay, I won't hurt you.
It's okay.

541
01:02:33,500 --> 01:02:37,545
- Where the hell did she come from?
- I found her hiding.

542
01:02:37,671 --> 01:02:39,547
She saw what happened here.

543
01:03:06,700 --> 01:03:09,661
Are you ready to go home,
Mrs. Murdoch?

544
01:03:09,786 --> 01:03:12,539
The Inspector is gonna take me.
Thanks.

545
01:03:37,147 --> 01:03:38,565
It's beautiful.

546
01:03:41,151 --> 01:03:45,613
It was a gift from my mother.
She died recently.

547
01:03:46,948 --> 01:03:49,367
I keep it with me
to remind me of her.

548
01:03:51,328 --> 01:03:52,662
I'm sorry.

549
01:03:56,082 --> 01:03:58,168
It's a funny thing, though.

550
01:03:58,293 --> 01:04:00,670
I can't remember
when she gave it to me.

551
01:04:03,214 --> 01:04:06,217
How do you think
I could forget a thing like that?

552
01:04:08,219 --> 01:04:12,557
Do you think about the past much,
Mrs. Murdoch?

553
01:04:14,726 --> 01:04:16,561
What's happening, Inspector?

554
01:04:17,854 --> 01:04:20,231
I'm not sure I know anymore.

555
01:04:23,693 --> 01:04:26,321
I left your old room like it was.

556
01:04:26,446 --> 01:04:28,490
You can sleep here tonight, huh?

557
01:04:28,615 --> 01:04:32,786
Glad to have you back, Johnny,
even if it is for just a night.

558
01:04:32,911 --> 01:04:34,704
- Karl.
- Huh?

559
01:04:34,829 --> 01:04:37,415
- Is that the right time?
- Why, sure.

560
01:04:37,540 --> 01:04:39,918
That clock's kept perfect time
since I bought it.

561
01:04:40,043 --> 01:04:44,339
- Am or pm?
- What do you think, Johnny?

562
01:04:44,464 --> 01:04:46,341
I don't understand.
How can it be night already?

563
01:04:46,466 --> 01:04:50,845
- What happened to the day? How'd I miss it?
- You're tired. Anything's possible.

564
01:04:50,970 --> 01:04:56,393
Look, uh... get some sleep.
We'll straighten all this out tomorrow.

565
01:05:22,252 --> 01:05:25,004
"Guide to Shell Beach"
by Johnny Murdoch.

566
01:05:27,549 --> 01:05:28,800
Oh, come on.

567
01:05:48,194 --> 01:05:50,613
- Hello?
- Emma, he's here.

568
01:05:50,738 --> 01:05:54,868
- 'He's acting mighty peculiar.'
- I know. He's not himself.

569
01:05:54,993 --> 01:05:57,203
Keep him there,
and I'll be right over.

570
01:05:57,328 --> 01:05:59,456
- I'll try.
- Thanks, Karl. Bye.

571
01:06:01,833 --> 01:06:02,834
Johnny!

572
01:06:02,959 --> 01:06:06,337
I can't promise anything, Mrs. Murdoch.
We just have to play it as it comes.

573
01:06:06,463 --> 01:06:09,924
Johnny, we...
We just wanted to help! Ha!

574
01:06:16,681 --> 01:06:20,101
Karl... Uncle Karl.

575
01:06:20,226 --> 01:06:24,230
Johnny, if you're in some kind of trouble,
maybe we could do something.

576
01:06:25,190 --> 01:06:29,694
Haven't seen you in so long, yes.

577
01:07:31,965 --> 01:07:33,216
Ah!

578
01:07:42,392 --> 01:07:47,730
Mr. Murdoch, you've been the cause
of much distress.

579
01:07:51,734 --> 01:07:54,237
- Start talking.
- There's no need for this.

580
01:07:54,362 --> 01:07:57,699
There's no escape.
The city's ours. We made it.

581
01:07:57,824 --> 01:07:59,284
What are you talking about?

582
01:07:59,409 --> 01:08:02,287
We fashioned this city
on stolen memories,

583
01:08:02,412 --> 01:08:06,165
different eras, different pasts,
all rolled into one.

584
01:08:06,291 --> 01:08:10,420
Each night, we revise it, refine it,
in order to learn.

585
01:08:10,545 --> 01:08:13,339
- Learn what?
- About you, Mr. Murdoch.

586
01:08:13,464 --> 01:08:17,969
You and your fellow inhabitants,
what makes you human.

587
01:08:18,094 --> 01:08:21,431
- Why?
- We need to be like you.

588
01:08:22,140 --> 01:08:24,225
I understand you now, Mr. Murdoch.

589
01:08:24,350 --> 01:08:27,979
I remember that which you do not,
what you've been missing.

590
01:08:28,104 --> 01:08:33,318
The ocean, yes,
running along the waves as a child,

591
01:08:33,443 --> 01:08:38,906
meeting Emma at the river,
that first kiss that followed.

592
01:08:39,032 --> 01:08:41,909
- What are you?
- You've seen what we are.

593
01:08:42,035 --> 01:08:44,871
We use your dead as vessels.

594
01:09:28,414 --> 01:09:31,292
Ah! Ah!

595
01:09:51,938 --> 01:09:53,856
Aargh!

596
01:10:06,119 --> 01:10:07,704
Aargh! Aargh!

597
01:10:54,584 --> 01:10:56,127
Get in!

598
01:11:11,017 --> 01:11:14,896
- What about her? Ring any bells?
- She was alive when I left her.

599
01:11:15,021 --> 01:11:17,857
- What about this?
- This is nothing. This is...

600
01:11:17,982 --> 01:11:20,443
It must mean something,
all those pictures.

601
01:11:20,568 --> 01:11:23,654
What pictures?
I don't understand this.

602
01:11:23,780 --> 01:11:26,532
Stop playing games with me, Murdoch!
Stop telling me lies!

603
01:11:26,657 --> 01:11:30,328
- I am not telling you lies!
- Help me out here. Make me understand.

604
01:11:30,453 --> 01:11:32,455
I have this jigsaw puzzle
in front of my face,

605
01:11:32,580 --> 01:11:35,875
and every time I try to rearrange
the pieces, it still doesn't make any sense.

606
01:11:36,000 --> 01:11:39,253
You think it makes sense to me?
I'm as much in the dark here as you are.

607
01:11:39,378 --> 01:11:41,422
You let me ask you a question?

608
01:11:42,548 --> 01:11:44,717
- You heard of a place called Shell Beach?
- Sure.

609
01:11:44,842 --> 01:11:46,719
- You know how to get there?
- Yeah.

610
01:11:46,844 --> 01:11:49,055
- You tell me?
- Ah, alright.

611
01:11:49,180 --> 01:11:52,016
You just, uh...
You go to the...

612
01:11:53,017 --> 01:11:56,187
- Where? Where do you go?
- Just give me a second, will you?

613
01:11:58,105 --> 01:12:00,024
You can't remember, can you?

614
01:12:02,401 --> 01:12:04,862
You think that's kind of odd? Hmm?

615
01:12:06,030 --> 01:12:08,241
Wait, I got a better one for you.

616
01:12:08,366 --> 01:12:12,495
When was the last time you remember
doing something during the day?

617
01:12:12,620 --> 01:12:15,873
- What do you mean?
- I just mean, during the day.

618
01:12:16,833 --> 01:12:19,585
Daylight. When was the last time
you remember seeing it?

619
01:12:19,710 --> 01:12:23,297
And I'm not talking about some distant,
half-forgotten childhood memory.

620
01:12:23,422 --> 01:12:26,551
I mean, like, yesterday?
Last week? When?

621
01:12:26,676 --> 01:12:28,970
Can you come up
with a single memory?

622
01:12:30,304 --> 01:12:31,806
You can't, can you?

623
01:12:31,931 --> 01:12:37,854
You know something, I don't think
the sun even exists in this place.

624
01:12:37,979 --> 01:12:40,565
Cos I've been up for hours
and hours and hours,

625
01:12:40,690 --> 01:12:42,900
and the night never ends here.

626
01:12:43,025 --> 01:12:45,194
- That is crazy.
- You're damn right this is crazy.

627
01:12:45,319 --> 01:12:47,905
But listen to me, Bumstead.
It's not just me.

628
01:12:48,030 --> 01:12:51,576
It's all of us.
They're doing something to all of us.

629
01:12:51,701 --> 01:12:54,412
- Shut up, you... I've heard enough.
- Please listen.

630
01:12:54,537 --> 01:12:56,163
Shut up, will you? Shut up!

631
01:13:06,507 --> 01:13:10,136
There has to be an explanation
for this somewhere.

632
01:13:17,727 --> 01:13:19,312
Explain this.

633
01:13:39,790 --> 01:13:42,001
John, I'm so sorry.

634
01:13:43,127 --> 01:13:45,963
I never meant to hurt you, John.
And I did it, and I don't know why I did.

635
01:13:46,088 --> 01:13:50,760
- I wish I could take it all back.
- No, no. Emma, you didn't do it.

636
01:13:50,885 --> 01:13:54,847
This affair of yours, whatever it is,
the thing you're supposed to have done,

637
01:13:54,972 --> 01:13:56,933
you didn't do it,
I don't believe it ever happened.

638
01:13:57,058 --> 01:14:00,686
- What do you mean?
- I know this is gonna sound crazy...

639
01:14:00,811 --> 01:14:03,898
But what if we never knew
each other before now?

640
01:14:04,023 --> 01:14:07,026
What if the first time we ever met

641
01:14:07,151 --> 01:14:10,237
was last night in your...
in our apartment,

642
01:14:10,363 --> 01:14:12,949
and everything you remember,

643
01:14:13,074 --> 01:14:16,077
and everything that I'm supposed
to remember, never really happened?

644
01:14:16,202 --> 01:14:18,704
Someone just wants us
to think it did.

645
01:14:23,834 --> 01:14:25,878
You know, back in the apartment...

646
01:14:26,963 --> 01:14:29,715
...I suddenly felt like
I didn't know you at all.

647
01:14:31,258 --> 01:14:33,761
It was as if you were a stranger.

648
01:14:36,013 --> 01:14:41,060
But how can that be true?
I so vividly remember meeting you.

649
01:14:42,395 --> 01:14:44,939
I remember falling in love with you.

650
01:14:46,399 --> 01:14:48,526
I remember losing you.

651
01:14:48,651 --> 01:14:50,069
Time's up.

652
01:14:50,987 --> 01:14:53,823
No, wait, wait. Please, just...

653
01:14:54,657 --> 01:14:56,659
Just one more minute?

654
01:15:02,206 --> 01:15:04,417
I love you, John.

655
01:15:07,169 --> 01:15:09,463
You can't fake something like that.

656
01:15:10,881 --> 01:15:12,717
No, you can't.

657
01:15:40,327 --> 01:15:43,956
- Inspector? Sir?

658
01:15:45,624 --> 01:15:48,335
Detective Walenski
killed himself last night.

659
01:15:48,461 --> 01:15:52,131
I, um... kind of thought
you should know.

660
01:15:54,800 --> 01:15:57,428
Oh, and the chief wanted
to see you.

661
01:15:58,637 --> 01:15:59,972
Sir?

662
01:16:05,936 --> 01:16:07,855
I knew you'd track the killer down, sir.

663
01:16:19,241 --> 01:16:21,202
Um, sir...

664
01:16:23,329 --> 01:16:24,538
Shoelace.

665
01:16:37,718 --> 01:16:40,888
- How can we help you, sir?
- You can sleep.

666
01:16:43,724 --> 01:16:46,060
- Gentlemen...
- Sleep. Sleep.

667
01:16:49,730 --> 01:16:51,148
Evening, sir.

668
01:16:59,740 --> 01:17:00,950
Bumstead.

669
01:17:03,828 --> 01:17:07,832
I wanted to be the first
to congratulate you about the case.

670
01:17:10,084 --> 01:17:12,878
Take us to Murdoch.

671
01:17:13,003 --> 01:17:14,421
Sleep.

672
01:17:41,699 --> 01:17:45,953
- Dr. Schreber.
- I knew you'd come eventually.

673
01:17:46,078 --> 01:17:48,539
Don't you think it's about time
you started giving me some answers?

674
01:17:48,664 --> 01:17:50,916
Yes. Yes, of course.

675
01:17:51,041 --> 01:17:53,085
Won't you please sit down?

676
01:17:55,921 --> 01:17:57,840
I come here quite often.

677
01:17:57,965 --> 01:18:00,342
It's one of the few places
I'm allowed a moment's peace.

678
01:18:00,467 --> 01:18:03,804
You see, they have
an aversion to water.

679
01:18:04,889 --> 01:18:07,224
One could almost call it a phobia.

680
01:18:07,349 --> 01:18:10,686
Who are they?
What do they want from me?

681
01:18:10,811 --> 01:18:12,271
Right.

682
01:18:12,396 --> 01:18:14,398
Well, for now, let's just say

683
01:18:14,523 --> 01:18:17,067
that you were the subject
of their experiment.

684
01:18:17,193 --> 01:18:19,111
We all are.

685
01:18:20,571 --> 01:18:24,867
You're not crazy, John.
And you're not a murderer.

686
01:18:27,286 --> 01:18:30,497
I'm sorry about this, I truly am,
but we do not have much time,

687
01:18:30,623 --> 01:18:33,292
and I cannot afford the luxury
of doing this the right way.

688
01:18:33,417 --> 01:18:37,087
Everything you need to know,
all the answers are in this syringe.

689
01:18:37,213 --> 01:18:40,799
I need you to inject yourself.
It's the only way to make you understand.

690
01:18:40,925 --> 01:18:43,385
- You're kidding me, right?
- We're running out of time, John.

691
01:18:43,510 --> 01:18:47,223
- You have to do this now.
- Give me the gun, Doctor.

692
01:18:47,348 --> 01:18:49,683
Inspector, he is more disturbed
than we thought.

693
01:18:49,808 --> 01:18:52,436
I may not be the judge of personality
that you are, Doctor,

694
01:18:52,561 --> 01:18:56,065
but you're the one
who looks disturbed to me.

695
01:18:56,190 --> 01:18:58,359
You do not know
what you are doing.

696
01:18:59,401 --> 01:19:03,155
What exactly is in this, Doctor?

697
01:19:04,740 --> 01:19:08,035
All the answers you've been looking for,
John, I swear to you.

698
01:19:08,160 --> 01:19:13,666
Oh... Guess I'll just have
to hold on to it for safekeeping.

699
01:19:14,625 --> 01:19:17,670
- If you don't mind.
- Let's go, Doctor.

700
01:19:18,545 --> 01:19:21,924
Go? Where are we going?

701
01:19:22,049 --> 01:19:23,968
Shell Beach.

702
01:19:25,052 --> 01:19:27,388
That's where you want to go, isn't it?

703
01:19:27,513 --> 01:19:29,014
The ocean.

704
01:19:37,982 --> 01:19:42,903
Why are you doing this?
What do you hope to gain by helping me?

705
01:19:43,028 --> 01:19:44,488
The truth.

706
01:19:45,155 --> 01:19:48,242
None of these maps extend
far enough to show the ocean.

707
01:19:48,367 --> 01:19:51,620
You won't find anything there,
| promise you! I've been there and...

708
01:19:51,745 --> 01:19:54,915
If you've been there, you can show us
the way, then, can't you?

709
01:19:55,040 --> 01:19:56,917
We'll see for ourselves.

710
01:19:58,168 --> 01:20:02,631
I won't. I refuse.
You can't make me go there!

711
01:20:15,477 --> 01:20:17,604
You were saying, Doctor?

712
01:20:29,283 --> 01:20:32,619
I don't understand.
Used to be a bridge here.

713
01:20:39,418 --> 01:20:42,338
Try that again, your friends are gonna
be fishing you out of the canal!

714
01:20:42,463 --> 01:20:45,841
Don't hurt me. I'll tell you everything.
It doesn't matter anymore anyway.

715
01:20:52,681 --> 01:20:54,433
What?

716
01:20:57,019 --> 01:20:58,979
Who are you people?

717
01:21:03,150 --> 01:21:07,654
We will give you some more
pretty things soon... Anna.

718
01:21:07,780 --> 01:21:09,531
I'm not Anna.

719
01:21:11,200 --> 01:21:13,369
You will be soon, yes.

720
01:21:17,206 --> 01:21:20,042
I have another use for her first.

721
01:21:26,590 --> 01:21:28,926
First, there was darkness.

722
01:21:30,094 --> 01:21:31,970
Then came the Strangers.

723
01:21:34,139 --> 01:21:36,683
They abducted us
and brought us here.

724
01:21:37,726 --> 01:21:41,855
This city, everyone in it,
is their experiment.

725
01:21:41,980 --> 01:21:44,650
They mix and match our memories
as they see fit,

726
01:21:44,775 --> 01:21:48,070
trying to divine
what makes us unique.

727
01:21:48,195 --> 01:21:50,823
One day, a man might be
an inspector.

728
01:21:50,948 --> 01:21:54,034
The next, someone entirely different.

729
01:21:54,159 --> 01:21:57,371
When they want to study
a murderer, for instance,

730
01:21:57,496 --> 01:22:02,292
they simply imprint one of their citizens
with a new personality,

731
01:22:02,418 --> 01:22:08,215
arrange a family for him, friends,
an entire history, even a lost wallet.

732
01:22:09,007 --> 01:22:11,385
Then they observe the results.

733
01:22:11,510 --> 01:22:16,014
Will a man, given the history
of a killer, continue in that vein?

734
01:22:16,140 --> 01:22:19,852
Or are we, in fact, more than
the mere sum of our memories?

735
01:22:19,977 --> 01:22:25,065
This business of you being a killer
was an unhappy coincidence.

736
01:22:25,190 --> 01:22:27,818
You have had dozens of lives
before now.

737
01:22:27,943 --> 01:22:32,114
You just happened to wake up
while I was imprinting you with this one.

738
01:22:32,239 --> 01:22:34,283
Why are they doing all of this?

739
01:22:34,408 --> 01:22:37,327
It is our capacity for individuality,

740
01:22:37,453 --> 01:22:40,164
our souls that makes us
different from them.

741
01:22:40,289 --> 01:22:42,458
They think they can find
the human soul,

742
01:22:42,583 --> 01:22:45,878
if they understand
how our memories work.

743
01:22:46,003 --> 01:22:49,923
All they have are collective memories.
They share one group mind.

744
01:22:50,048 --> 01:22:54,845
They're dying, you see? Their entire race
is on the brink of extinction.

745
01:22:54,970 --> 01:22:56,889
They think we can save them.

746
01:22:57,014 --> 01:23:00,017
- Where do I fit in?
- You are different, John.

747
01:23:00,142 --> 01:23:02,853
You resisted my attempt
to imprint you.

748
01:23:07,399 --> 01:23:10,486
Somehow you have developed
their ability to tune.

749
01:23:10,611 --> 01:23:13,780
That is how they change things.
That is how they built this city.

750
01:23:13,906 --> 01:23:17,075
They have machines buried deep
beneath the surface,

751
01:23:17,201 --> 01:23:20,704
that allow them to focus
their telepathic energies.

752
01:23:21,622 --> 01:23:24,041
"They control everything here,
even the sun.'

753
01:23:24,166 --> 01:23:27,169
That's why it's always dark,
they can't stand the light.

754
01:23:27,294 --> 01:23:28,795
So, why do they need you?

755
01:23:28,921 --> 01:23:32,758
'When they first brought us here,
they extracted what was in us.'

756
01:23:32,883 --> 01:23:37,054
So they could store the information,
remix it like so much paint,

757
01:23:37,179 --> 01:23:39,640
and give us back new memories
of their choosing.

758
01:23:39,765 --> 01:23:43,310
But they still needed an artist
to help them.

759
01:23:45,145 --> 01:23:48,106
I understood the intricacies
of the human mind

760
01:23:48,232 --> 01:23:50,526
better than they ever could.

761
01:23:50,651 --> 01:23:55,113
So, they allowed me to keep my skills
as a scientist, because they needed them.

762
01:23:55,239 --> 01:23:57,616
But they made me delete
everything else.

763
01:24:06,124 --> 01:24:10,587
Can you imagine what it is like
being forced to erase your own past?

764
01:24:10,712 --> 01:24:14,800
What about my past?
What about my childhood?

765
01:24:14,925 --> 01:24:19,096
Shell Beach, Uncle Karl...
What about this?

766
01:24:20,597 --> 01:24:24,268
- This was blank when I found it!
- You still don't understand, John.

767
01:24:24,393 --> 01:24:27,479
You were never a boy,
not in this place.

768
01:24:27,604 --> 01:24:32,234
Your entire history is an illusion,
a fabrication, as it is with all of us.

769
01:24:32,359 --> 01:24:35,028
You made those drawings happen
with your gift.

770
01:24:35,153 --> 01:24:37,489
You say they brought us here.

771
01:24:38,699 --> 01:24:40,826
From where?

772
01:24:43,579 --> 01:24:46,748
I'm sorry.
I don't remember.

773
01:24:47,749 --> 01:24:50,043
None of us remember that,

774
01:24:50,168 --> 01:24:54,172
what we once were,
what we might have been,

775
01:24:54,298 --> 01:24:56,133
somewhere else.

776
01:25:13,150 --> 01:25:17,195
I've taken you this far.
You don't need me anymore.

777
01:25:17,321 --> 01:25:18,947
Let's go.

778
01:25:37,174 --> 01:25:39,176
John...

779
01:26:03,283 --> 01:26:05,952
There is no ocean, John.

780
01:26:06,912 --> 01:26:09,206
There is nothing beyond the city.

781
01:26:10,707 --> 01:26:13,168
The only place home exists...

782
01:26:15,337 --> 01:26:17,339
...Is In your head.

783
01:26:25,681 --> 01:26:28,975
No! No! John, stop!

784
01:26:31,895 --> 01:26:32,979
No!

785
01:26:35,774 --> 01:26:37,109
Stop!

786
01:26:42,698 --> 01:26:45,409
Please! No!

787
01:27:04,428 --> 01:27:06,555
And now you know the truth.

788
01:27:25,991 --> 01:27:27,159
No!

789
01:28:15,207 --> 01:28:17,793
You'll allow yourself to surrender,
Mr. Murdoch,

790
01:28:17,918 --> 01:28:20,086
or it will result
in this one's death, yes?

791
01:28:20,212 --> 01:28:24,090
What do I care? She's not my real wife!
She's nothing to me!

792
01:28:24,216 --> 01:28:28,345
But you do still care, don't you,
Mr. Murdoch?

793
01:28:28,470 --> 01:28:33,099
You see, I have become the monster
you were intended to be.

794
01:28:34,893 --> 01:28:38,605
Shall I end her life now,
as you would have?

795
01:28:42,067 --> 01:28:43,902
Don't hurt her, please!

796
01:28:44,027 --> 01:28:47,489
Then surrender, Mr. Murdoch.

797
01:28:48,990 --> 01:28:50,408
Sleep.

798
01:28:52,744 --> 01:28:54,412
Now.

799
01:28:56,331 --> 01:28:57,958
John?

800
01:29:13,014 --> 01:29:14,140
Sleep.

801
01:29:35,453 --> 01:29:38,874
The doctor was right.
He has evolved.

802
01:29:38,999 --> 01:29:40,750
Kill him.

803
01:29:40,876 --> 01:29:43,253
Kill him! Kill him!

804
01:29:46,590 --> 01:29:49,259
Kill him! Kill him!

805
01:29:52,012 --> 01:29:54,514
He is powerful, yes.

806
01:29:54,639 --> 01:29:59,019
Dangerous. But he can also lead us
to what we seek.

807
01:29:59,144 --> 01:30:02,439
What the doctor calls the soul.

808
01:30:02,564 --> 01:30:06,192
It is time for our experiment
to move into a final phase.

809
01:30:06,318 --> 01:30:08,945
We no longer need
the other subjects.

810
01:30:09,070 --> 01:30:12,574
The time for study is over.

811
01:30:12,699 --> 01:30:17,495
It is time to be one with John Murdoch.

812
01:30:23,627 --> 01:30:26,963
It is time, Doctor.
Imprint.

813
01:30:29,758 --> 01:30:32,510
Shut it down!

814
01:30:32,636 --> 01:30:35,180
Shut it down forever!

815
01:30:49,861 --> 01:30:51,863
What are you doing?

816
01:30:51,988 --> 01:30:56,451
They want to imprint you
with their own collective memories.

817
01:30:57,827 --> 01:31:03,083
They want to make you one of them,
so they can share your soul.

818
01:31:08,088 --> 01:31:10,382
Imprint, Doctor.
No more disobedience.

819
01:31:10,507 --> 01:31:15,261
I'm sorry, John.
The pain will only last a moment.

820
01:31:17,472 --> 01:31:18,473
No!

821
01:31:26,606 --> 01:31:28,108
Remember, John.

822
01:31:29,401 --> 01:31:31,444
"You'll rise to greater heights
than that, my boy.

823
01:31:31,569 --> 01:31:34,114
'One day when you're older,
you'll understand.'

824
01:31:34,990 --> 01:31:37,325
- Remember.

825
01:31:37,450 --> 01:31:40,245
"That's it, John.
Practice makes perfect.

826
01:31:40,370 --> 01:31:43,456
'Now remember what I told you.
Never talk to strangers.'

827
01:31:43,581 --> 01:31:45,208
John, remember.

828
01:31:45,333 --> 01:31:48,336
'You're probably wondering
why I keep appearing in your memories.

829
01:31:48,461 --> 01:31:50,797
"It is because I have inserted
myself in them.

830
01:31:50,922 --> 01:31:54,801
'All of these memories have been fabricated
to teach you about the Strangers,

831
01:31:54,926 --> 01:31:57,804
'give you a lifetime of knowledge
in a single syringe.

832
01:32:00,765 --> 01:32:03,143
"You will survive, John.

833
01:32:03,268 --> 01:32:07,355
"You will find strength within yourself,
and you will prevail.'

834
01:32:07,480 --> 01:32:08,732
Remember.

835
01:32:09,649 --> 01:32:11,651
<i>- 'Hi, Uncle Karl!
- 'Johnny"</i>

836
01:32:14,612 --> 01:32:16,740
"You're getting the hang of it, John.

837
01:32:16,865 --> 01:32:18,742
'Maybe one day,
I'll be working for you.'

838
01:32:19,701 --> 01:32:23,121
"This is the machine the Strangers use
to amplify their thoughts.

839
01:32:23,246 --> 01:32:26,332
'The machine that changes their world.
You must take control of it.

840
01:32:26,458 --> 01:32:28,376
"You must make the machine yours.

841
01:32:28,501 --> 01:32:32,630
'I know you can beat them, John.
But you must concentrate.'

842
01:32:34,924 --> 01:32:36,843
Something's wrong.

843
01:32:40,638 --> 01:32:42,265
Aargh!

844
01:32:44,684 --> 01:32:48,772
'There is no time for romance, John.
The world can be what you make it.'

845
01:32:49,814 --> 01:32:51,775
What have you done?

846
01:32:51,900 --> 01:32:56,780
<i>"You have the power to make
anything happen, but you must act now!</i>

847
01:37:40,521 --> 01:37:42,273
I knew you could do it, John.

848
01:37:42,398 --> 01:37:46,027
You have their power now.
You control their machines.

849
01:37:46,152 --> 01:37:47,653
Where's Emma?

850
01:37:48,488 --> 01:37:52,658
She's not Emma anymore, John.
She has been re-imprinted.

851
01:37:52,784 --> 01:37:54,952
- So give her back her memories.
- I can't.

852
01:37:55,078 --> 01:37:59,082
The facility where the Strangers stored
the memories has been destroyed.

853
01:37:59,916 --> 01:38:01,751
I'm sorry.

854
01:38:02,418 --> 01:38:04,879
What are you going to do now, John?

855
01:38:06,589 --> 01:38:09,717
You told me I had the power,
didn't you?

856
01:38:10,510 --> 01:38:13,721
I can make these machines
do anything I want.

857
01:38:14,806 --> 01:38:17,266
Make this world
anything I want it to be.

858
01:38:18,142 --> 01:38:22,271
Just so long as I concentrate
hard enough.

859
01:39:46,230 --> 01:39:48,232
Where are you going?

860
01:39:50,526 --> 01:39:51,986
Shell Beach.

861
01:40:32,902 --> 01:40:34,278
- What's the fare?
- Quarter.

862
01:40:40,535 --> 01:40:41,869
Oh, please.

863
01:40:41,994 --> 01:40:44,121
- Oh, no...
- It's fine.

864
01:40:44,247 --> 01:40:45,957
- Thank you.
- Okay.

865
01:41:25,705 --> 01:41:27,582
John...

866
01:41:31,294 --> 01:41:35,131
Been waiting for you, yes?
What are you doing?

867
01:41:35,256 --> 01:41:38,217
I'm just making a few little changes
around here, is all.

868
01:41:38,342 --> 01:41:43,139
- Are we sure that's what we want?
- I'm prepared to take my chances.

869
01:41:43,264 --> 01:41:47,810
I'm dying, John. Your imprint
Is not agreeable with my kind.

870
01:41:49,312 --> 01:41:54,358
But I wanted to know
what it was like... how you feel.

871
01:41:54,483 --> 01:41:57,111
You know how I was supposed to feel.

872
01:41:57,236 --> 01:42:00,948
That person isn't me.
Never was.

873
01:42:01,073 --> 01:42:04,702
You wanted to know what it was
about us that made us human.

874
01:42:06,120 --> 01:42:09,624
Well, you're not going
to find it in here.

875
01:42:11,125 --> 01:42:13,377
You went looking in the wrong place.

876
01:43:52,226 --> 01:43:54,103
It's so beautiful here.

877
01:43:59,316 --> 01:44:00,943
So bright.

878
01:44:06,949 --> 01:44:09,660
Do you know if Shell Beach
is round here?

879
01:44:09,785 --> 01:44:12,121
I think that's it, just over there.

880
01:44:30,431 --> 01:44:32,475
I'm headed that way myself.

881
01:44:34,435 --> 01:44:36,479
Would you like to join me?

882
01:44:38,898 --> 01:44:40,649
Sure.

883
01:44:45,237 --> 01:44:46,989
I'm Anna, by the way.

884
01:44:47,865 --> 01:44:49,992
- What's your name?
- John.

885
01:44:52,328 --> 01:44:53,871
John Murdoch.

